9. Orthogonality

The primitives are not perfectly orthogonal in the strict mathematical sense. They overlap.

Operational orthogonality means:

> Each primitive contributes a distinct transformation not reducible to the others without significant loss.

We can estimate orthogonality using effect vectors.

Let:

[
v_i =
(\Delta \text{attention},
\Delta \text{memory},
\Delta \text{identity},
\Delta \text{emotion},
\Delta \text{behavior},
\Delta \text{transfer})
]

Then:

[ O_{ij}

1 -
\frac{|\langle v_i,v_j\rangle|}
{|v_i||v_j|}
]

Large (O_{ij}) means the primitives produce distinct effects.

────────

10. Moneybin Graduation Rule

A primitive or artifact graduates into the Moneybin when it demonstrates:

1. Compression — high semantic leverage.
2. Reconstruction — can regenerate useful structure.
3. Translation — survives at least three renderings.
4. Generativity — produces valid new applications.
5. Provenance — preserves origin and development.
6. Calibration — separates observation, inference, and speculation.
7. Ethical robustness — remains defensible under scrutiny.

────────

